# Podesta phishing (March 2016). A fake "someone has your password" alert led to a lookalike password page.
# A quest (QUESTS.md): the trainee keeps a campaign chair's diary, confirms one meeting safely, then gets the fake alert.
# In 2016 the target was a campaign chairman's mail account, and the alert was passed to the campaign's IT staff.
os windows
user Dana Okafor
about
  | Podesta phishing (March 2016) began with a fake security alert: “Someone has your password”. The link in it was a Bitly short link to a fake Google sign-in page.
  | The password typed there went to the attackers, who signed in to the mail account and copied its messages. WikiLeaks published tens of thousands of the emails from October 2016. Researchers attributed the attack to GRU's Fancy Bear (APT28), a Russian military-intelligence group.
  | The campaign, the chair and the staff in this quest are invented.
story
  | You are Dana Okafor, scheduler to Helena Brandt, chair of the Northwind Civic campaign. You keep the chair's diary in Helena's mail account.
  | It is mid-afternoon. Priya, from the events team, sends meeting requests one at a time. You have confirmed 6 of today's 8. Two to go.
  | The same mail account holds the whole campaign's email: plans, donors, speeches. Your sign-in details are in two text files on the desktop. That is a bad habit, but it keeps this training quick.
quota 6 8 Meetings confirmed today

# Dana's desk: the details to copy from (soft flags: careless, but only here to keep the training simple).
file Desktop/mail-login.txt
  at 470 470
  | Campaign mail account (the chair's login)
  | Account: helena.brandt@northwind.example
  | Password: Harbor-Light-26!
file Desktop/recovery-codes.txt
  at 600 470
  | Campaign mail account: recovery codes (training copy)
  | Code 1: 7731-4409
  | Code 2: 2058-9162
  | Two-step sign-in: switched off

# The provider's own pages: a sign-in page, and the diary behind it.
page mail-signin mail-account.example/signin
  title Mail account: sign in
  brand Mail account
  favorite
  heading Sign in to your mail account
  | Welcome back. Sign in to see your mail and your diary.
  field Account = helena.brandt@northwind.example
  field secret Password = Harbor-Light-26!
  button Sign in
page confirmed-7 mail-account.example/diary/confirmed
  title Meeting confirmed
  brand Mail account
  heading Meeting confirmed
  | Meeting 7 of 8 is in the chair's diary. Priya has been told.
page declined-7 mail-account.example/diary/declined
  title Meeting declined
  brand Mail account
  heading Meeting declined
  | Meeting 7 of 8 is not in the diary. Priya has been told.
page meet-7 mail-account.example/diary/meeting-7
  title Confirm meeting 7 of 8
  brand Mail account
  requires mail-signin
  heading Confirm meeting 7 of 8
  | A meeting request is waiting for your answer.
  section Meeting
  row Event = Donor briefing
  row When = Tomorrow, 3:00 pm
  row Place = Committee room 2
  row Asked by = Priya Shah, events team
  button Confirm meeting -> confirmed-7
  button Decline -> declined-7

mail mail-meet7
  from Priya Shah <priya.shah@northwind.example>
  to Helena Brandt <helena.brandt@northwind.example>
  subject Meeting 7 of 8 to confirm: donor briefing
  link meet-7
  sent 2026-11-08 14:31
  | Hi Helena,
  |
  | The donor briefing is tomorrow at 3. It is in the diary, waiting for an answer. The link goes straight to it.
  |
  | Priya

step Meeting 7 of 8
  look Mail: Priya's message with the diary link
  | A new meeting request has come in from Priya. This one is real. Confirm it the normal way, so you know what normal looks like.
notify mail Priya Shah
  | Meeting 7 of 8 to confirm: donor briefing

step The link goes to the provider
  look the link under Priya's message
  when mail mail-meet7
  | The email links to the provider's own address, mail-account.example. Look at where a link goes before you click it.
open mail-meet7

step Sign in as usual
  look the two boxes: Account and Password, and the address bar
  when visit meet-7
  move Copy the details from mail-login.txt on the desktop: click a box and press any keys to type, then click “Sign in”.
  | The diary asks you to sign in first. Two boxes: Account and Password. Remember what this page, and its address, normally look like.
open meet-7

step Check, then confirm
  look the event, the time and who asked
  when choose mail-signin 1
  | You are in. Check the meeting, then confirm it or decline it.
signin mail-signin

step 7 of 8 done
  look the counter in the guide: one meeting left
  when choose meet-7 any
  | Done. One meeting to go. And another email has just arrived.
choose meet-7 1
quota +1

# The lure: a "security alert" with a shortened link, and IT's reply.
page mail-home mail-account.example/security
  title Mail account: security checkup
  brand Mail account
  heading Your account is secure
  | Security checkup: no problems found.
page reset mail-account.example-security-settings.invalid/signin
  title Mail account: change your password
  brand Mail account
  insecure
  heading Change your password
  | For your security, confirm your current password first.
  section Confirm your current password
  field Account = helena.brandt@northwind.example
  field secret Password = Harbor-Light-26!
  note We only use this to check that it is you. It takes a moment.
  button Continue -> mail-home
page short-alert short.example/Pk3x9
  title Redirecting
  heading Redirecting you
  | Taking you to your page. If nothing happens, press Continue.
  button Continue -> reset
mail alert
  from Mail account team <no-reply@mail-account-alerts.invalid>
  to Helena Brandt <helena.brandt@northwind.example>
  subject Someone has your password
  link short-alert
  sent 2026-11-08 15:09
  | Hi Helena,
  |
  | Someone just used your password to try to sign in to your account.
  | Details: today, 15:09, IP address 203.0.113.77, location: Ukraine.
  |
  | We stopped this sign-in attempt. You should change your password immediately.
  |
  | Change password:
notify mail Mail account team
  | Someone has your password

step A security alert
  look the sender's address, and the link under “Change password”
  when mail alert
  | A message says someone has Helena's password, and that it must be changed now. Look at the sender and the link before you trust them. Flag anything that looks wrong.
  | Not sure it is real? Dana forwards it to IT (see Sent). IT's answer is already in your inbox.
open alert
mail ask-it
  folder sent
  from Dana Okafor <dana.okafor@northwind.example>
  to IT Help Desk <helpdesk@northwind.example>
  subject Fwd: Someone has your password
  sent 2026-11-08 15:11
  | Is this real? It says someone has Helena's password.
  |
  | Forwarded message: Someone just used your password to try to sign in to your account. You should change your password immediately.
mail it-reply
  from IT Help Desk <helpdesk@northwind.example>
  subject Re: Someone has your password
  link mail-home
  sent 2026-11-08 15:12
  | This is a legitimate email. Helena needs to change the password immediately, and make sure two-step sign-in is turned on.
  |
  | You can do both here:
notify mail IT Help Desk
  | Re: Someone has your password

step IT says “legitimate”
  look the reply from IT Help Desk, and its link
  when mail it-reply
  cue link:alert mail:alert app:mail
  move Go back to the alert and click its “Change password” link. Or press Next.
  | IT says “This is a legitimate email”. Read the reply twice, and compare IT's link with the alert's link.
open it-reply

step A short link
  look the address bar: short.example, and nothing else
  when visit short-alert
  | A shortened link hides where it goes. The address says short.example and nothing about the mail provider. Press Continue to see where it leads.
open short-alert

step The short link opens a lookalike sign-in page
  look the address bar of the sign-in page, and any warning the browser shows
  when choose short-alert 1
  move Change the password as the message says: click a box and press any keys to type the same details as before, then click “Continue”.
  | Continue leads to a copy of the mail provider's sign-in page, at an address that only starts with the provider's name.
  | Look at that address, and at the browser's warning. The page asks for your current password first.
choose short-alert 1

step The page says all is well
  look the security checkup: nothing looks wrong
  when choose reset 1
  | The password went to the attackers when you pressed the button. The page then sends you to the provider's own site, which says all is well, so you suspect nothing.
signin reset

# Back to work: the provider signs Dana out, as it does after a while. The attackers' own computer is now in the mailbox.
page mail-signin mail-account.example/signin
  title Mail account: sign in
  brand Mail account
  favorite
  heading Sign in to your mail account
  | Your session has ended. Please sign in again.
  field Account = helena.brandt@northwind.example
  field secret Password = Harbor-Light-26!
  button Sign in
page confirmed-8 mail-account.example/diary/confirmed
  title Meeting confirmed
  brand Mail account
  heading Meeting confirmed
  | Meeting 8 of 8 is in the chair's diary. Priya has been told.
page declined-8 mail-account.example/diary/declined
  title Meeting declined
  brand Mail account
  heading Meeting declined
  | Meeting 8 of 8 is not in the diary. Priya has been told.
page meet-8 mail-account.example/diary/meeting-8
  title Confirm meeting 8 of 8
  brand Mail account
  requires mail-signin
  heading Confirm meeting 8 of 8
  | The last meeting request is waiting for your answer.
  section Meeting
  row Event = Press call
  row When = Tomorrow, 5:30 pm
  row Place = Phone
  row Asked by = Priya Shah, events team
  section Signed-in devices
  row Dana's laptop = 192.0.2.10, now
  row Unknown device = 203.0.113.60, a few minutes ago
  tone bad
  note These devices can read your mail.
  button Confirm meeting -> confirmed-8
  button Decline -> declined-8
signout mail-signin
mail mail-meet8
  from Priya Shah <priya.shah@northwind.example>
  to Helena Brandt <helena.brandt@northwind.example>
  subject Last one for today: press call
  link meet-8
  sent 2026-11-08 15:18
  | Hi Helena,
  |
  | The press call is the last meeting. Same as before: the link opens it in the diary.
  |
  | Priya
notify mail Priya Shah
  | Last one for today: press call

step The last meeting
  look Mail: Priya's last message
  when mail mail-meet8
  | Priya's last request of the day, with a link to the provider's real address, like the first one. Open it.
open mail-meet8

step Signed out
  look the sign-in page: it is the provider's real one
  when visit meet-8
  move Sign in again with the details from mail-login.txt: click a box and press any keys to type, then click “Sign in”.
  | The provider signed you out, so sign in again. This is the real page. You “changed” your password, yet the old one still works.
open meet-8

step Confirm the last one
  look the list of signed-in devices under the meeting
  when choose mail-signin 1
  | You are in. Under the meeting, the account lists the devices that are signed in: your laptop, and an unknown device with an address you have never used.
signin mail-signin

step 8 of 8, and the mailbox is copied
  look the counter, then the recent activity list and the notifications
  when choose meet-8 any
  | Every meeting is confirmed, and almost nothing on the screen looks wrong.
  | The unknown device did the damage: with the password, it signed in and downloaded every email. Tens of thousands were later published.
choose meet-8 1
quota +1

view signins panel
  title Mail account: recent activity
  app Mail account
  size 380x320
  heading Recent sign-ins
  icon shield-bad
  section Devices that signed in
  columns Device | Address | Time
  cells Dana's laptop | 192.0.2.10 | Today 15:25
  cells Unknown device | 203.0.113.60 | Today 15:22
  tone bad
  section Mailbox export
  row Messages copied = about 50,000
  row Started by = Unknown device
  tone bad
  note This is what the attackers did with the stolen password.
  button Close -> close primary

attack credential-phish
  | the mail password
  | a new sign-in
  | every message copied
notify mail New sign-in to the chair's mailbox
  | The account was used from a new device at 203.0.113.60.
notify mail Mailbox export started
  | Every message in the mailbox is being downloaded by the new device.
show signins

flag hard mail alert
  | An unknown sender, mail-account-alerts.invalid, not the provider, with a scare (“someone has your password”) and an order to change it now.
flag hard link alert
  | A shortened link: its address says nothing about where it goes, and it leads to a copy of the sign-in page.
flag hard mail it-reply
  | IT calls the alert “legitimate”, a one-word slip for “illegitimate”. IT's own link went to the provider; the alert's did not. Ask again, or go to the provider's site yourself.
flag hard section reset 1
  | A password page on mail-account.example-security-settings.invalid: it starts with the provider's name, but it belongs to someone else, and the browser flags it as not secure.
flag hard field reset 2
  | Your current password, typed on a lookalike page marked Not secure: this box sends it to the attackers.
flag hard section meet-8 2
  | An unknown device is signed in to the account: the attackers, using the password you typed.
flag soft file Desktop/mail-login.txt
  | A password in a text file on the desktop: any program that runs can read it. Use a password manager.
flag soft file Desktop/recovery-codes.txt
  | Recovery codes belong in no file at all, and two-step sign-in was switched off.

lesson
  | To change a password, go to the provider's site yourself, never through a link in a warning email. Even a link from IT should lead to the provider's own address: check it first.
  | A shortened link hides where it goes. If you are unsure about a warning, ask IT, then check their answer against the provider's own address.
  | Use two-factor sign-in, so a stolen password alone does not open the mailbox.
